# Scan a video into a style

The kit is at `{{KIT}}`; its command is `{{KIT}}/.venv/bin/stylekit`.

## 1. Scan

Ask only for what is missing: the reference (file path or link) and a name for the style. Everything else is measured.
Any notes the user gives about the style go in `--notes` word for word.

```bash
{{KIT}}/.venv/bin/stylekit scan "<file or link>" [more references...] --name "<style name>" [--notes "..."]
```
- One reference works; 3-5 make the numbers medians instead of single readings (say which you had).
- Links: YouTube works as is. Instagram/TikTok usually need a login: add `--cookies chrome` (or safari/firefox), or
  ask the user to download the video and pass the file.
- Time: about 12-15 s of Mac time per second of reference (a 30 s reel is ~6-8 min; a long video measures its first
  120 s, ~25-30 min, unless `--max-seconds` says otherwise). Run it in the background, tell the user it is working
  and roughly how long, and wait for it to finish.
- Cost: free; everything runs on the Mac. `--gemini` adds an optional naming pass (needs `GEMINI_API_KEY`, a few cents).

## 2. Finish the style (you look at it)

Follow `{{KIT}}/WRITING_A_STYLE.md`: read the contact sheets, set the font/colours in `recipe.json`, check them with
`stylekit calibrate <style>`, and write `STYLE.md`. The scan created the skill link `~/.claude/skills/style-<style>`;
new sessions list it as `/style-<style>`.

## 3. Edit their footage in it

If they gave footage (now or later), follow `{{KIT}}/EDITING.md` with `--style <style>`.

## If something fails

`{{KIT}}/.venv/bin/stylekit doctor` names every missing piece and the command that fixes it. Setup from scratch:
`{{KIT}}/SETUP.md`.
